{
  "teamId": 134,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Pittsburgh Pirates were a team in transition, a young, rebuilding club that never truly threatened respectability in the standings but offered flashes of genuine excitement amid a long, difficult summer. Opening April at 9-12, the Pirates showed the inconsistency that would define their season — moments of promise punctuated by the kind of late-inning collapses that accompany a roster still finding itself. The month set the tone: not hopeless, but not yet coherent.\n\nMay and early June brought the brightest stretch of the season. Jack Suwinski emerged as an electric presence, his bat providing some of the campaign's most memorable moments, while Ke'Bryan Hayes and Michael Chavis chipped in timely blows. The Pirates played .500 ball in May (12-15, respectable for this club) before June turned cruel, a 10-18 month that saw the bullpen bleed leads and opposing bats do their worst against relievers like Wil Crowe and David Bednar. The gap between what this team could be and what it was became painfully visible.\n\nJuly and August were the heart of a long losing slog. Pittsburgh went 9-17 and 9-19 in those months, and the high-leverage moments captured in this season's most impactful plays skewed dark — grand slams, late-inning singles that blew leads, and a bullpen that could not hold games when it mattered most. Yet even here, Bryan Reynolds quietly compiled an All-Star caliber season, and younger pieces like Oneil Cruz offered glimpses of a better future. The Pirates were not so much collapsing as they were simply outmatched night after night.\n\nSeptember offered a mild reprieve — a 10-17 record that actually represented a slight improvement in winning percentage — and the Pirates closed with a winning October at 3-2. By year's end, the 62-100 record and a run differential of minus-226 told the full story of a team that allowed far more than it scored, relied too heavily on a shaky pitching staff (4.66 ERA), and could not generate offense (a .655 OPS). The 2022 Pirates were, in a phrase, a team building toward something better while enduring the necessary pain of the present.",
  "chapters": [
